{"meta":{"chapter":"01","section":"03","title":"Relative Motion","course":"dynamics","description":"Use reference frames correctly, compute relative velocity in one and two dimensions, and connect frame choice to real navigation problems.","learning_objectives":["Calculate relative velocity between two moving objects","Apply the Galilean transformation for velocities","Analyze relative motion in 2D","Solve practical problems involving relative motion"],"concept_ids":["relative_velocity","reference_frame","galilean_transform"],"physics_prereqs":["rectilinear_motion","curvilinear_motion"],"math_prereqs":["vectors"],"units_focus":["m/s"],"common_errors":["frame","direction","sign"],"estimated_time_minutes":15,"prereq_section_ids":["dynamics/01/01"],"practice_ids":["relative_1d","relative_approach","relative_2d"]},"content":"# Relative Motion\n\nOn final approach, a pilot lines up with a runway but points the nose noticeably to one side. To an observer on the ground the aircraft tracks straight down the centerline, yet the fuselage is \"crabbed\" into the wind. Nothing is wrong with the controls. The pilot is solving a relative-motion problem in real time: the velocity through the air and the velocity over the ground are two different vectors, and only one of them keeps the wheels on the runway.\n\nAll motion is relative. A train can be moving at high speed relative to the ground while a passenger quietly sipping coffee feels almost at rest relative to the seat. In engineering the key question is never just \"How fast is it moving?\" but rather:\n\n**Moving relative to what frame of reference?**\n\nThat one question matters in highway safety, drone navigation, robotics, river crossing, spacecraft docking, and collision analysis. Getting the frame wrong is one of the most common ways a kinematics calculation can be internally consistent yet physically meaningless.\n\n---\n\n## Learning Objectives\n\nBy the end of this section, you will be able to:\n\n1. **State** the relative-velocity equation and explain what each frame means physically\n2. **Apply** signed subtraction to one-dimensional relative-motion problems\n3. **Resolve** planar relative motion into components and recombine magnitude and direction\n4. **Solve** translating-frame problems such as river crossing, wind correction, and closing rate\n5. **Extend** the relative formulation to acceleration between translating frames\n\n---\n\n## The Core Equation\n\nThe velocity of particle B relative to particle A is:\n\n$$\n\\boxed{\\vec{v}_{B/A} = \\vec{v}_B - \\vec{v}_A}\n$$\n\nRead this carefully as \"the velocity of B as observed by someone riding on A.\" Both $\\vec{v}_B$ and $\\vec{v}_A$ are measured in the same fixed (ground) frame; their difference is what the moving observer on A actually sees.\n\nAn equivalent rearrangement is:\n\n$$\n\\vec{v}_B = \\vec{v}_A + \\vec{v}_{B/A}\n$$\n\nThis addition form is the workhorse. It says a ground velocity is the velocity of a moving platform plus the velocity measured relative to that platform. When you chain frames together, the relative subscripts cancel like fractions:\n\n$$\n\\vec{v}_{B/G} = \\vec{v}_{B/A} + \\vec{v}_{A/G}\n$$\n\nThe \"$A$\" cancels, leaving B relative to the ground. This bookkeeping rule is what keeps multi-frame problems (boat, water, shore or plane, air, ground) from collapsing into nonsense.\n\n> **Key Insight:** Relative motion is vector subtraction, not speed subtraction. Direction is carried at every step, even in one dimension where it appears only as a sign.\n\n---\n\n## Why Reference Frames Matter\n\nReference frames are not decorative labels. They change the meaning of the numbers.\n\n- A cyclist moving at $8 \\text{ m/s}$ relative to the road is at rest relative to the bicycle seat.\n- A boat moving north in still water has a different ground velocity once a river current pushes it east.\n- A sensor mounted on a robot arm may see a moving part as stationary relative to one joint and fast-moving relative to the room.\n\nIf you mix frames accidentally, the algebra can look correct while the answer is physically wrong. In this chapter we assume every frame is **translating** (no rotation), so velocities simply add and subtract. Rotating frames introduce extra terms (the transport and Coriolis terms) that belong to a later chapter.\n\n| Notation | Meaning |\n| --- | --- |\n| $\\vec{v}_A$, $\\vec{v}_B$ | Velocities measured in the fixed ground frame |\n| $\\vec{v}_{B/A}$ | Velocity of B as seen by an observer moving with A |\n| $\\vec{v}_{A/G}$ | Velocity of A relative to ground (same as $\\vec{v}_A$) |\n\nThe subscript grammar is the part students misread most. Match each relative-motion symbol or rule to its meaning to lock in the \"as observed by\" convention before the worked examples.\n\n<sequence-builder mode=\"match\" items='[{\"term\":\"v_B/A\",\"definition\":\"Velocity of B as seen by an observer riding on A (v_B minus v_A)\"},{\"term\":\"v_A/G\",\"definition\":\"Velocity of A relative to the fixed ground, the same as v_A\"},{\"term\":\"v_B/G = v_B/A + v_A/G\",\"definition\":\"Frame chaining: the inner A subscript cancels, leaving B relative to ground\"},{\"term\":\"v_B/A = 0\",\"definition\":\"B appears stationary to A because their ground velocities are identical\"}]'></sequence-builder>\n\n---\n\n## Relative Motion in 1D\n\nFor straight-line motion the equation reduces to signed subtraction. Choose a positive direction first, then attach signs before any arithmetic.\n\n### Worked Example 3.1: Two Cars on a Highway\n\n**Problem:** Car A travels east at $25 \\text{ m/s}$; Car B travels west at $20 \\text{ m/s}$ on the same road. What is the velocity of B relative to A?\n\n**Step 1: Choose a frame.** Let east be positive, so $v_A = +25 \\text{ m/s}$ and $v_B = -20 \\text{ m/s}$.\n\n**Step 2: Apply the relative equation.**\n\n$$\nv_{B/A} = v_B - v_A = (-20) - (+25) = -45 \\text{ m/s}\n$$\n\n**Step 3: Interpret.**\n\n$$\nv_{B/A} = \\boxed{-45 \\text{ m/s}}\n$$\n\nTo the driver of Car A, Car B approaches at $45 \\text{ m/s}$ from the east. The negative sign does not mean the speed is \"bad\"; it tells you the relative motion points opposite the chosen positive direction. This closing speed, not either car's individual speed, is what governs collision energy and required stopping distance.\n\n---\n\n## Relative Motion in 2D\n\nWhen motion is planar, treat each component separately:\n\n$$\n\\vec{v}_{B/A} = (v_{Bx} - v_{Ax})\\,\\hat{i} + (v_{By} - v_{Ay})\\,\\hat{j}\n$$\n\nThe magnitude and direction follow from the components:\n\n$$\n|\\vec{v}_{B/A}| = \\sqrt{(v_{Bx} - v_{Ax})^2 + (v_{By} - v_{Ay})^2}, \\qquad \\theta = \\tan^{-1}\\!\\left(\\frac{v_{By}-v_{Ay}}{v_{Bx}-v_{Ax}}\\right)\n$$\n\nThe explorer below makes the subtraction tangible. Enter the ground-frame velocity components of two cars approaching an intersection and read the relative-velocity components and the closing speed $|\\vec{v}_{B/A}|$ that a collision-avoidance system would compute. Try equal velocities to see the relative speed collapse to zero.\n\n<parameter-explorer config='{\"title\":\"Relative velocity of B with respect to A (closing speed)\",\"sliders\":[{\"name\":\"vAx\",\"label\":\"Car A east component vAx\",\"min\":-40,\"max\":40,\"step\":1,\"unit\":\"m/s\",\"default\":30},{\"name\":\"vAy\",\"label\":\"Car A north component vAy\",\"min\":-40,\"max\":40,\"step\":1,\"unit\":\"m/s\",\"default\":0},{\"name\":\"vBx\",\"label\":\"Car B east component vBx\",\"min\":-40,\"max\":40,\"step\":1,\"unit\":\"m/s\",\"default\":0},{\"name\":\"vBy\",\"label\":\"Car B north component vBy\",\"min\":-40,\"max\":40,\"step\":1,\"unit\":\"m/s\",\"default\":40}],\"outputs\":[{\"label\":\"Relative east comp (vBx - vAx)\",\"unit\":\"m/s\",\"expr\":\"vBx - vAx\"},{\"label\":\"Relative north comp (vBy - vAy)\",\"unit\":\"m/s\",\"expr\":\"vBy - vAy\"},{\"label\":\"Closing speed |v_B/A|\",\"unit\":\"m/s\",\"expr\":\"sqrt((vBx - vAx)^2 + (vBy - vAy)^2)\"}],\"plot\":{\"x\":\"vBy\",\"output\":2}}'></parameter-explorer>\n\n<inline-check\n  question=\"Two cars have IDENTICAL ground velocities (same speed, same direction). What does the relative-velocity explorer report for the closing speed |v_B/A|?\"\n  conceptId=\"relative_velocity\"\n  explanation=\"Relative velocity is v_B minus v_A. If the two velocity vectors are identical, their difference is the zero vector, so the closing speed is 0. Each driver sees the other as stationary, even though both move fast over the ground.\"\n  options='[{\"text\":\"Zero, because the velocity vectors cancel in the subtraction.\",\"correct\":true},{\"text\":\"The sum of the two speeds.\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"Twice either car speed.\",\"correct\":false}]'></inline-check>\n\n### Worked Example 3.2: Aircraft in Wind\n\n**Problem:** A plane has airspeed $200 \\text{ m/s}$ due north (velocity relative to the air). The wind blows $50 \\text{ m/s}$ due east (air relative to ground). Find the plane's ground velocity.\n\n**Step 1: Chain the frames.**\n\n$$\n\\vec{v}_{plane/ground} = \\vec{v}_{plane/air} + \\vec{v}_{air/ground} = (50)\\hat{i} + (200)\\hat{j} \\ \\text{m/s}\n$$\n\n**Step 2: Magnitude.**\n\n$$\n|\\vec{v}| = \\sqrt{50^2 + 200^2} = \\boxed{206.2 \\text{ m/s}}\n$$\n\n**Step 3: Direction.**\n\n$$\n\\theta = \\tan^{-1}\\!\\left(\\frac{50}{200}\\right) = \\boxed{14.0^\\circ \\ \\text{east of north}}\n$$\n\nThe plane points straight north through the air but drifts $14^\\circ$ off course over the ground. To hold a true northward track the pilot must crab the nose west into the wind, which is exactly the next problem.\n\n### Worked Example 3.3: River Crossing (Drift and Correction)\n\n**Problem:** A boat can move at $4 \\text{ m/s}$ relative to the water. A river $200 \\text{ m}$ wide flows east at $3 \\text{ m/s}$. The pilot first aims the bow straight across (due north). (a) Find the ground velocity, crossing time, and downstream drift. (b) Find the heading needed to land directly opposite the start.\n\n**Part (a): Bow aimed straight across.** Let east be $\\hat{i}$ and north be $\\hat{j}$.\n\n$$\n\\vec{v}_{boat/ground} = \\vec{v}_{boat/water} + \\vec{v}_{water/ground} = (3)\\hat{i} + (4)\\hat{j} \\ \\text{m/s}\n$$\n\n$$\n|\\vec{v}| = \\sqrt{3^2 + 4^2} = \\boxed{5.0 \\text{ m/s}}\n$$\n\nThe northward component is unaffected by the east-flowing current, so the crossing time depends only on the $4 \\text{ m/s}$ across-stream speed:\n\n$$\nt = \\frac{200 \\text{ m}}{4 \\text{ m/s}} = \\boxed{50 \\text{ s}}\n$$\n\nDuring those 50 s the current carries the boat downstream:\n\n$$\nd = (3 \\text{ m/s})(50 \\text{ s}) = \\boxed{150 \\text{ m east}}\n$$\n\n**Part (b): Land directly opposite.** Now aim the bow at angle $\\phi$ west of north so the east component of the boat cancels the current. Setting the net east velocity to zero:\n\n$$\n-\\,(4)\\sin\\phi + 3 = 0 \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad \\sin\\phi = \\frac{3}{4} \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad \\phi = \\boxed{48.6^\\circ \\ \\text{west of north}}\n$$\n\nThe remaining north component gives the crossing speed and time:\n\n$$\nv_{north} = 4\\cos(48.6^\\circ) = 2.65 \\text{ m/s}, \\qquad t = \\frac{200}{2.65} = \\boxed{75.6 \\text{ s}}\n$$\n\nCorrecting for drift costs about $26 \\text{ s}$ of extra crossing time, because part of the boat's capability is now spent fighting the current instead of advancing across. This trade between landing accuracy and crossing speed is the same one a ferry operator, a swimmer, and a station-keeping ROV all face.\n\nWalk the river-crossing logic step by step. Predict each result before revealing it, and notice exactly where the drift-correction heading steals speed from the across-stream direction.\n\n<step-reveal title=\"River crossing: drift, then correction (Example 3.3)\" steps='[{\"prompt\":\"The bow is aimed straight across (north) at 4 m/s in water that flows east at 3 m/s. What is the boat ground SPEED?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"5.0 m/s\",\"1.0 m/s\",\"7.0 m/s\"],\"answerIndex\":0,\"reveal\":\"Ground velocity = 3 i + 4 j, so speed = sqrt(3^2 + 4^2) = 5.0 m/s. The current adds an east component the boat never intended.\"},{\"prompt\":\"The river is 200 m wide. The crossing time depends on which component of the boat velocity?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"The 4 m/s northward (across-stream) component only\",\"The full 5 m/s ground speed\",\"The 3 m/s current\"],\"answerIndex\":0,\"reveal\":\"Only the across-stream component moves the boat toward the far bank: t = 200/4 = 50 s. The east current does not shorten the crossing; it just slides the boat downstream.\"},{\"prompt\":\"During those 50 s, how far downstream (east) does the current carry the boat?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"150 m east\",\"100 m east\",\"50 m east\"],\"answerIndex\":0,\"reveal\":\"d = (3 m/s)(50 s) = 150 m east. This is the drift you must cancel to land directly opposite.\"},{\"prompt\":\"To land straight across, aim the bow at angle phi west of north so 4 sin(phi) cancels the 3 m/s current. What is phi?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"48.6 degrees west of north\",\"36.9 degrees west of north\",\"30 degrees west of north\"],\"answerIndex\":0,\"reveal\":\"sin(phi) = 3/4, so phi = 48.6 degrees west of north. Part of the 4 m/s is now spent fighting the current.\"},{\"reveal\":\"The remaining north component is 4 cos(48.6) = 2.65 m/s, so t = 200/2.65 = 75.6 s. Correcting for drift costs about 26 s: accuracy and crossing speed trade against each other.\"}]'></step-reveal>\n\n---\n\n## Relative Acceleration\n\nDifferentiating the velocity relation with respect to time gives the matching acceleration rule for translating frames:\n\n$$\n\\vec{a}_{B/A} = \\vec{a}_B - \\vec{a}_A\n$$\n\nA useful consequence: if two vehicles have the **same** acceleration (for example, both in identical free fall, or both braking at the same rate), then $\\vec{a}_{B/A} = 0$ and B appears to move at constant velocity relative to A even though both are accelerating in the ground frame. This is why a dropped object inside a uniformly accelerating elevator still falls in a straight line relative to the cabin, just with an altered apparent gravity.\n\n### Worked Example 3.4: Closing Rate at an Intersection\n\n**Problem:** Car A heads east at $30 \\text{ m/s}$; Car B heads north at $40 \\text{ m/s}$, approaching the same intersection. Find the velocity of B relative to A.\n\n$$\n\\vec{v}_{B/A} = \\vec{v}_B - \\vec{v}_A = (0 - 30)\\hat{i} + (40 - 0)\\hat{j} = (-30)\\hat{i} + (40)\\hat{j} \\ \\text{m/s}\n$$\n\n$$\n|\\vec{v}_{B/A}| = \\sqrt{(-30)^2 + 40^2} = \\boxed{50 \\text{ m/s}}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\theta = \\tan^{-1}\\!\\left(\\frac{40}{-30}\\right) = \\boxed{126.9^\\circ \\ \\text{from east (i.e. north-west)}}\n$$\n\nTo the driver of A, car B does not merely approach from the left; it sweeps in along a line $126.9^\\circ$ from due east at $50 \\text{ m/s}$. Collision-avoidance and radar systems compute exactly this relative vector, because a constant relative bearing with nonzero closing speed is the classic signature of an impending collision.\n\n---\n\n## Practical Engineering Uses\n\nRelative motion shows up whenever two moving systems interact.\n\n| Application | Frames Involved | Why It Matters |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Autonomous driving | Vehicle vs. surrounding traffic | Closing rate sets braking and avoidance decisions |\n| Mobile robotics | Tool tip vs. moving conveyor | Pick-and-place must track the part, not the floor |\n| Marine and ferry routing | Hull vs. water vs. shore | Current correction sets heading and crossing time |\n| Aircraft navigation | Airframe vs. air vs. ground | Wind correction angle keeps the ground track true |\n| Spacecraft docking | Chaser vs. target | Only the relative state vector matters for capture |\n\nA lot of \"surprise\" failures in motion analysis come from forgetting that the chosen frame quietly changes the answer.\n\n## How These Ideas Connect\n\nRelative motion rests on three linked ideas. Select a node to see how the reference frame, the relative-velocity vector, and the Galilean transformation depend on one another.\n\n<concept-map concepts='[\"relative_velocity\",\"reference_frame\",\"galilean_transform\"]' title=\"Relative Motion\"></concept-map>\n\n<dynamic-scenario topic=\"Relative Motion in Navigation\" context=\"An autonomous rescue drone must deliver medicine to a boat moving downstream while crosswinds push the drone sideways.\"></dynamic-scenario>\n\n---\n\n## Common Errors\n\n- **Subtracting speeds instead of velocities:** direction must be included; $|\\vec{v}_B| - |\\vec{v}_A|$ is almost never $|\\vec{v}_{B/A}|$.\n- **Switching frames mid-problem:** for example, mixing air-relative and ground-relative values in the same equation.\n- **Ignoring signs in 1D:** approaching and separating cases can flip quickly if directions are not defined first.\n- **Forgetting the subscript-cancellation rule:** the inner frame must match ($\\vec{v}_{B/A}+\\vec{v}_{A/G}$), or the chained result is meaningless.\n\n---\n\n## Summary\n\n### Key Equations\n\n| Concept | Equation |\n| --- | --- |\n| Relative velocity | $\\vec{v}_{B/A} = \\vec{v}_B - \\vec{v}_A$ |\n| Velocity addition (frame chaining) | $\\vec{v}_{B/G} = \\vec{v}_{B/A} + \\vec{v}_{A/G}$ |\n| Planar components | $\\vec{v}_{B/A} = (v_{Bx}-v_{Ax})\\hat{i} + (v_{By}-v_{Ay})\\hat{j}$ |\n| Relative acceleration | $\\vec{a}_{B/A} = \\vec{a}_B - \\vec{a}_A$ |\n\n### Key Takeaways\n\n- Velocity descriptions depend on the observer's frame; always name the frame first.\n- Relative motion is vector subtraction, with direction preserved even in 1D as a sign.\n- Frame subscripts cancel like fractions, which keeps multi-frame chains consistent.\n- For translating frames the same rule applies to acceleration; equal accelerations cancel.\n- Closing rate, not absolute speed, governs collisions, docking, and avoidance.\n\n---\n\n## Check Your Understanding\n\n<interactive-quiz\n  question=\"A runner moves east at 6 m/s on a moving walkway that itself moves east at 2 m/s relative to the ground. What is the runner's velocity relative to the ground?\"\n  conceptId=\"relative_velocity\"\n  options='[{\"text\":\"8 m/s east, because the walkway and runner velocities add in the same direction.\",\"isCorrect\":true},{\"text\":\"4 m/s east, because the walkway speed must always be subtracted.\",\"isCorrect\":false},{\"text\":\"6 m/s east, because only the runner motion counts for relative motion.\",\"isCorrect\":false}]'\n  explanation=\"Correct. When the motions are in the same direction, the ground-relative velocity is the platform velocity plus the motion measured relative to that platform.\"></interactive-quiz>\n\n<interactive-quiz\n  question=\"Why is it dangerous to solve a boat-in-current problem using the boat speed through water as if it were already the ground speed?\"\n  conceptId=\"reference_frame\"\n  options='[{\"text\":\"Because water-relative speed and ground-relative speed belong to different reference frames and cannot be treated as interchangeable.\",\"isCorrect\":true},{\"text\":\"Because boats only follow scalar equations, not vector equations.\",\"isCorrect\":false},{\"text\":\"Because currents affect acceleration but never velocity.\",\"isCorrect\":false}]'\n  explanation=\"Correct. A boat's speed relative to water is not the same as its speed relative to shore. The current changes the frame, so the velocity must be recombined vectorially.\"></interactive-quiz>\n\n---\n\n## Looking Ahead\n\nRelative motion closes the kinematics chapter by reminding us that velocity descriptions depend on perspective. The next step in dynamics is to ask not just how motion looks, but **what forces make it happen**, which brings us to Newton's second law and kinetics.\n","simulation":null,"illustration":null,"practice":{"problems":[{"id":"relative_1d","type":"numeric","stem":"Car A moves east at 30 m/s, Car B moves east at 20 m/s. Find the velocity of B relative to A.","final_answer":{"value":-10,"unit":"m/s","tolerance":0.1},"difficulty":"easy","concept_ids":["relative_velocity","galilean_transform"]},{"id":"relative_approach","type":"numeric","stem":"Train A moves north at 40 m/s, Train B moves south at 35 m/s. At what speed do they approach each other?","final_answer":{"value":75,"unit":"m/s","tolerance":0.1},"difficulty":"medium","concept_ids":["relative_velocity","reference_frame"]},{"id":"relative_2d","type":"numeric","stem":"An airplane has airspeed 250 m/s north. Wind is 80 m/s east. Find the ground speed magnitude.","final_answer":{"value":262.5,"unit":"m/s","tolerance":1},"difficulty":"hard","concept_ids":["relative_velocity","reference_frame","galilean_transform"]}]}}